This is the **Erdős–Straus conjecture** (Erdős Problem 242): for every integer $n > 2$, there exist distinct positive integers $1 \le x < y < z$ such that

$$\frac{4}{n} = \frac{1}{x} + \frac{1}{y} + \frac{1}{z}.$$

The Lean declaration is annotated `@[category research open, AMS 11]`, marking it as an **open research problem**.

This conjecture has been open since it was posed by Erdős in 1950. It has been verified computationally for all $n$ up to extremely large bounds (well beyond $10^{17}$), and various partial results are known (e.g., it holds for all $n$ not congruent to specific residue classes, for all $n$ in certain arithmetic progressions, density results, etc.), but **no complete proof exists**. The Schinzel generalization stated as the second theorem is even stronger and also open.

